Let me re-examine the history. The word game that became Scrabble was invented by **Alfred Mosher Butts**, an unemployed architect in New York. In the early 1930s he developed it as "Lexiko," then revised it into "Criss-Crosswords," doing the famous letter-frequency analysis from newspaper pages. James Brunot came in later: in 1948 he bought the rights from Butts, refined some rules, and renamed it "Scrabble" — but Brunot did not invent it.

My earlier answer incorrectly credited Brunot and also conflated the timeline (I even placed Criss-Crosswords in 1938 and Lexiko before it in ways that don't match the record). The inventor was Butts; Brunot was the manufacturer/marketer.

